{
 "ep": 8,
 "total": 140,
 "title": "Tables, Rows, Pages and Tuples",
 "phase": "Foundations",
 "lines": [
  {
   "say": "A table is not one big blob. It's a stack of pages.",
   "cap": "A table is a stack of *pages.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "This is episode eight of the PostgreSQL DBA series. Today: how your rows are really stored.",
   "cap": "Episode 8: how rows are *really* *stored.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "In SQL, you see a table as rows and columns. That is the logical view.",
   "cap": "SQL shows *rows* and *columns.* The logical view."
  },
  {
   "say": "On disk, every table and index is stored as an array of fixed-size pages, usually eight kilobytes each.",
   "cap": "On disk: an array of *fixed-size* *pages,* usually *8 kB.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "In a table, all pages are equal, so a row can be stored in any page.",
   "cap": "A row can live in *any* page.",
   "cue": "any"
  },
  {
   "say": "A row version stored in a page is called a tuple. Every update writes a new tuple.",
   "cap": "A stored row version is a *tuple.*",
   "cue": "tuple"
  },
  {
   "say": "Every page has five parts. First, a twenty-four byte page header, with pointers to the free space.",
   "cap": "*Page header:* 24 bytes, with free space pointers."
  },
  {
   "say": "Then an array of line pointers, four bytes each, pointing to the tuples.",
   "cap": "*Line pointers:* 4 bytes each, pointing to tuples."
  },
  {
   "say": "Line pointers grow from the start of the free space, and new tuples are added from the end.",
   "cap": "Pointers grow *down,* tuples grow *up* from the end.",
   "cue": "end"
  },
  {
   "say": "Last, the special space. Indexes use it. In ordinary tables, it is empty.",
   "cap": "*Special space:* for indexes, empty in tables."
  },
  {
   "say": "Each tuple starts with its own header, twenty-three bytes on most machines.",
   "cap": "Tuple header: *23 bytes* on most machines."
  },
  {
   "say": "It stores xmin, the inserting transaction, and xmax, the deleting one.",
   "cap": "*xmin* = inserting, *xmax* = deleting transaction.",
   "cue": "xmax"
  },
  {
   "say": "Let's see it on a real server. SHOW block_size returns eight one nine two bytes.",
   "cap": "*SHOW block_size* returns *8192.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "ctid is a row's physical address: page, then item. Row one is at page zero, item one.",
   "cap": "*ctid* = (page, item). Row 1 is at *(0,1).*",
   "cue": "address"
  },
  {
   "say": "I loaded one thousand small rows. pg_relation_size divided by eight one nine two gives six pages.",
   "cap": "1,000 small rows fill *6 pages.*",
   "cue": "six"
  },
  {
   "say": "And row one thousand lives in page five, item seventy-five.",
   "cap": "Row 1000 is at *(5,75).*"
  },
  {
   "say": "pageinspect shows the line pointers. Tuple one starts at offset eight one five two, near the end of the page.",
   "cap": "*pageinspect:* tuple 1 starts at offset *8152.*",
   "cue": "offset"
  },
  {
   "say": "The next tuples sit lower, filling the page backwards. Page zero holds one hundred eighty-five.",
   "cap": "Page 0 holds *185* tuples, filled *backwards.*",
   "cue": "hundred"
  },
  {
   "say": "Now update row two. Its ctid moves from page zero, item two, to page five, item seventy-six.",
   "cap": "After UPDATE, row 2 moves to *(5,76).*",
   "cue": "moves"
  },
  {
   "say": "Common mistake: using ctid as a row ID. It changes on UPDATE and VACUUM FULL. Use a primary key.",
   "cap": "*Never* use ctid as a row ID. Use a *primary* *key.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "A tuple can't span pages. Rows wider than about two kilobytes trigger TOAST, which compresses or moves big values out of line.",
   "cap": "Rows over ~*2 kB* trigger *TOAST.*",
   "cue": "TOAST"
  },
  {
   "say": "DBA takeaway: tables grow in whole pages. Size divided by block_size gives the page count.",
   "cap": "Size / block_size = *pages* in the table."
  },
  {
   "say": "Recap: tables are arrays of eight kilobyte pages, holding line pointers and tuples.",
   "cap": "Recap: *pages,* *line* *pointers,* *tuples.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "And ctid is page plus item. It changes, so identify rows by primary key.",
   "cap": "*ctid* changes. Use a *primary* *key.*",
   "cue": "changes"
  },
  {
   "say": "Next up: the PostgreSQL data types you should know. Follow so you don't miss it, and send this to a friend who's learning PostgreSQL.",
   "cap": "Next up: *data* *types.* *Follow* and share."
  }
 ],
